592186544754 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544754 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544754 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d5052787-b20b-5d33-ba51-bf4f48076c76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544756 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d6c931c3-cfba-5275-ba59-84cd3742ca6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544800 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544800 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/94/9405ee6a4b24ef36c2a59a9f4a984728716e9c281456d1459d591393d3c73720.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n$r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )},$\n  represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2be045cc-480f-57a5-b39f-c617f83c588d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544800 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal lies in the fourth quadrant. Since the petal lies in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta +\\pi $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta +\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,4\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},-\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},-\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies in the fourth quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544802 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544802 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544802 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544802 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d9/d9985c6afd3e5440dbe1e51e4046e7744c9a2e01472354054796247d40ed7ed6.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n$r=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$\nrepresented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544802 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3922fac2-663d-5efa-bc44-c819bbafb745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544802 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544804 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544804 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544804 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/446278a820ba21da7f5a1f15639e8cc4f96e38f38f3b5905b926bb9d2a106948.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544804 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3aec8613-8bc5-55d8-bb63-25dfe3e5bcce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544804 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544820 #edb/ref 1069 "q-360575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544820 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544820 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6f/6feb80156311c92f948b35742b39df3e6582c62a0d578481026b3141b01de3f