d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544849 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544849 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b7/b7475f0a169e62121711d52daaf51e8954053fc35738266d94240b6e70de6258.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544849 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96dc50ad-7c40-5c2b-a752-a6f1cca71c8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544849 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544860 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/cc41b37639eb7715fbecd55510222f77957c05b705bc83b93c755ad2e7cf3dcd.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n$r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$\n  represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a11f42c6-9c97-5af9-adee-a7210e67b825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544860 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,4\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{24},\\frac{\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{11\\pi }{24},\\frac{\\pi }{24}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-11\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544862 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/97/97b30cfa241a6e1b9db4d5332749ae503a926711c0e7c78ab188c2327463c89b.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9a8bb7e-ad2c-5185-87eb-ce30902a04e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544862 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
